/* What each alert kind needs in order to fire (#242). Not a header: no
 * include guard, on purpose. Every includer defines
 * X(ID, INPUT, FRAMES, BANDS, SHAPE, NEEDS) first and gets one expansion
 * per row.
 *
 * A sibling of sloth_alerts.def and not a new column there: that file
 * is in the contract, and sloth-mesh includes it with a two-argument X.
 * tests/test_rule_coverage.c holds this list to that one, row for row, in
 * the same order.
 *
 *   ID      the kind, as in sloth_alerts.def.
 *   INPUT   the one input the rule reads its evidence from (cov_input_t,
 *           src/rule_coverage.h, without the COV_IN_ prefix):
 *             DOT11    802.11 frames from a radio in monitor mode
 *             IP       packets of the IP capture (--iface)
 *             HOST     the host's own kernel tables: sockets, the
 *                      neighbour table, the interface list
 *             BLE_ADV  Bluetooth LE advertising, from --bt-monitor or
 *                      --bt-capture
 *             BLE_HCI  the host controller's own commands, --bt-monitor
 *                      only
 *             GNSS     the --gps receiver
 *             ADSB     the --adsb decoder
 *             SYS      sloth's own status sampler (/proc, /sys)
 *           A corroborating input a rule can use and does not need
 *           (EVIL_TWIN's wired check) is not declared.
 *   FRAMES  for DOT11, the frame classes the evidence is made of
 *           (COV_F_*, or-ed): BEACON (beacons and probe responses),
 *           PROBE (probe requests), AUTH, ASSOC ((re)association
 *           requests and responses), DEAUTH (and disassociation),
 *           ACTION, CTRL, DATA, EAPOL (EAPOL and EAP in data frames).
 *           0 for every other input.
 *   BANDS   for DOT11, the bands the rule is about (COV_B_*); ANY when
 *           it reads every band. ANY for every other input.
 *   SHAPE   for DOT11, what the evidence looks like in time, which
 *           decides what a hopping radio does to it:
 *             STATE     repeated or standing (a beacon's content): one
 *                       visit to the channel reads it
 *             RATE      a count inside a time window: time off the
 *                       channel lowers the count
 *             EVENT     one frame, sent once
 *             EXCHANGE  an ordered exchange of several frames
 *           NA for every other input.
 *   NEEDS   what the operator must have configured, or-ed (COV_N_*):
 *           OWN_NET (--my-ssid or --my-bssid), ROSTER (--known-mac or
 *           --known-macs), DB (--db). 0 for none.
 *
 * A declaration is a statement about the rule function, so it changes
 * with the rule: read src/alerts/ and the trigger column of
 * docs/views/alerts.md, never the kind's name. */
X(PORT_SCAN,                 HOST,    0, COV_B_ANY, NA, 0)
X(DEAUTH_FLOOD,              DOT11,   COV_F_DEAUTH, COV_B_ANY, RATE, 0)
X(NXDOMAIN_BURST,            IP,      0, COV_B_ANY, NA, 0)
X(THREAT_DOMAIN,             IP,      0, COV_B_ANY, NA, 0)
X(THREAT_IP,                 HOST,    0, COV_B_ANY, NA, 0)
